WEBVTT

NOTE SE301 Lesson 1: Size the model, then run the first endpoint — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.852
Your model fits on paper. Can your laptop still answer after you cancel its first stream?

2
00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:08.560
Start with a memory budget. Finish with two requests you can replay.

3
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.354
Quick question. Before you download a local model, what do you check?

4
00:00:13.354 --> 00:00:16.188
The product name? The memory on the sticker?

5
00:00:16.188 --> 00:00:18.828
Or the weight floor, with room left over?

6
00:00:18.828 --> 00:00:20.825
The floor, with room left over.

7
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:22.950
The sticker is the whole machine.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.888
Start with the weight floor.

9
00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:28.122
Parameter count, times bits per weight, divided by eight.

10
00:00:28.122 --> 00:00:30.788
That is gigabytes of weights, and only weights.

11
00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:34.589
Eighty billion parameters, at four-bits, starts at forty gigabytes.

12
00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:37.823
Three hundred twenty billion starts at one hundred sixty.

13
00:00:37.823 --> 00:00:41.000
Four hundred eighty billion starts at two hundred forty.

14
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.258
The floor is not the whole bill.

15
00:00:43.258 --> 00:00:49.937
Add metadata, the runtime, the operating system, your other programs, and a context cache for each open session.

16
00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:56.020
A two hundred forty gigabyte floor is a poor everyday choice on a two hundred fifty six gigabyte host.

17
00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:06.612
Load a small model first. On an Apple silicon Mac you already own, try a three to eight billion parameter model, four-bit, only if memory permits.

18
00:01:06.612 --> 00:01:14.118
The playbook's first fixture, on its forty eight gigabyte laptop, is Qwen2.5 Coder, 7B, Instruct, four-bit.

19
00:01:14.118 --> 00:01:17.345
If gpt-oss-20b is already working, compare it.

20
00:01:17.345 --> 00:01:19.870
Don't download it in order to start.

21
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.478
These Studio candidates need testing on the actual hardware.

22
00:01:23.478 --> 00:01:26.030
Keep your small laptop baseline for comparison.

23
00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.522
When it loads, run one prompt at 4K, at 16K, and at 32K.

24
00:01:30.522 --> 00:01:36.426
Write down peak memory, swap, time to first token, and output tokens per second.

25
00:01:36.426 --> 00:01:39.230
Stop when the wait misses your target.

26
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.822
Then build the endpoint. Write down the machine and the free disk.

27
00:01:43.822 --> 00:01:47.678
Install one pinned llmster runtime, and one model that fits.

28
00:01:47.678 --> 00:01:51.984
Bind it to loopback. One two seven point zero point zero point one.

29
00:01:51.984 --> 00:01:56.933
Not the open network. Send a completion, a stream, a cancel, and a tool call.

30
00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:59.118
Save them with the model manifest.

31
00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.560
A chat reply does not prove the tools.

32
00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.742
Try this on your own endpoint.

33
00:02:03.742 --> 00:02:08.566
Ask for the numbers one through twenty, one per line, with streaming turned on.

34
00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:11.436
Watch for the first piece before the final one.

35
00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:15.649
Cancel halfway. Then send a fresh request: reply with the word ready.

36
00:02:15.649 --> 00:02:18.397
Save what actually happened on both requests.

37
00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:23.098
A stopped display alone cannot tell you whether the server stopped computing.

38
00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:26.640
Check the runtime log before you mark that part as passed.

39
00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:33.360
One prompt on this page drives a computer-use agent, or a terminal agent, on a Mac or a PC.

40
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.610
It reads the operating system, the CPU, the GPU, the memory, and free disk.

41
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:44.280
It picks a tier. It installs llmster, or MLX on Apple silicon, or Ollama on a PC.

42
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.150
It sends one live request. Then it waits.

43
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.472
It waits before any admin install, any firewall change, and any sign-in.

44
00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:55.027
You name the action, and you say yes.

45
00:02:55.027 --> 00:02:59.446
Silence is not a yes. Don't paste a key, a token, or a password.

46
00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:02.209
Don't send a local file off the machine.

47
00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.661
The smoke test is one short sentence, to loopback.

48
00:03:05.661 --> 00:03:07.940
The reply stays on your computer.

49
00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:16.647
Rule one, for your agents. Before a download, write the weight floor, the memory you can spare, and the check that says the endpoint answered.

50
00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:19.119
Put that note where the agent can read it.

51
00:03:19.119 --> 00:03:22.650
Admin rights, a firewall change, or a sign-in: stop and ask.

52
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.870
Your turn. Open the prompt. Let the agent show you the tier it chose.

53
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.905
Say yes only to the install you understand.

54
00:03:30.905 --> 00:03:34.010
Keep the reply. That reply is your baseline.

55
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.959
Next in the playbook: a router in front of the models, with stable names like code-fast and review.

56
00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:41.090
See you in that lab.
